@sahmed73 I cannot answer any questions related to fix bond/react (that is @jrgissing 's domain), but you should provide the following information to make it easier to provide assistance:

which LAMMPS version are you using exactly? what platform are you running on? and what is the exact command line (i.e. how many MPI processes are you using, any accelerators/threads and how many)?

I didn’t see any mention of equilibration. For how long have you been running equilibration (without fix bond/react)? and did you encounter any warnings or other issues in the process? How did you confirm that your equilibration was sufficient?

@MatKie I will look into resolving the inconsistent behavior (LAMMPS should consistently refuse your calculation), but your model itself is bogus.

The long-range correction has **two** parts: 1) you have an `erfc()` based damping function in real space, so the interactions are smoothly reduced to zero at the cutoff, 2) you do a lattice sum in reciprocal space, which now also has to compensate for the damping and thus turns a conditionally convergent sum into a more easily converging sum (as fewer fourier components are required to converge smooth charge distributions than for point charges). In your model, you have significant double counting, since coul/cut is non-zero at the cutoff.
It is not possible to add dispersion correction to the mie potential. The best you can do is to increase the cutoff. Since the potential is much more short ranged than coulomb, it is not as bad an approximation, it just costs some performance. And you can add long-range coulomb with using the overlay with coul/long and a "regular" kspace style like pppm or ewald. This setup should have a **much** smaller error than combining coul/cut with kspace.

