@jfikar one possible issue with the INTEL package is, that it reorganizes a lot of data into special, restructured and aligned buffers to enable or encourage vectorization. So its memory requirements are higher than that without the intel suffix. Please also note that the largest consumer of memory allocation are typically the neighbor lists.

The non-numeric atom coords error is likely due to an overflow in the force computation. Your initial system has *very* high energy and pressure, so there is a good chance that your forces would overflow single precision computations. You can confirm this by switching to double precision calculation with `-pk 0 mode double`.

It would be helpful, if you could retool your input deck for a smaller system that would trigger the same kind of overflow with just 1 MPI process and post it. Then this could be used to try and identify which allocation overflows.

@jfikar please let us not mix up different problems.

The lost atoms and non-numeric atom coordinates are different issues from the memory allocation failure.

As you had already speculated, the memory allocation issue is a problem caused by a 32-bit integer overflow. I it is only indirectly related to the number of atoms, but more directly to the size of the neighbor lists.

Please try the following workaround:
```
 diff --git a/src/INTEL/intel_buffers.cpp b/src/INTEL/intel_buffers.cpp
 index 07e59b1bcf..bb54577097 100644
 --- a/src/INTEL/intel_buffers.cpp
 +++ b/src/INTEL/intel_buffers.cpp
 @@ -391,9 +391,10 @@ void IntelBuffers ::_grow_nbor_list(NeighList * /*list*/,
 free_nbor_list();
 _list_alloc_atoms = 1.10 * nlocal;
 int nt = MAX(nthreads, _off_threads);
 - int list_alloc_size = (_list_alloc_atoms + nt * 2 + pack_width - 1) *
 - get_max_nbors();
 - lmp->memory->create(_list_alloc, list_alloc_size, "_list_alloc");
 +
 + bigint list_alloc_size =
 + (bigint)(_list_alloc_atoms + nt * 2 + pack_width - 1) * (bigint)get_max_nbors();
 + _list_alloc = (int *) lmp->memory->smalloc(list_alloc_size * sizeof(int), "_list_alloc");
 #ifdef _LMP_INTEL_OFFLOAD
 if (offload_end > 0) {
 int * list_alloc =_list_alloc;
 @@ -706,7 +707,7 @@ double IntelBuffers ::memory_usage(const int nthreads)
 if (_off_f) tmem += fstride*_off_threads * sizeof(vec3_acc_t);
 #endif

 - tmem += (_list_alloc_atoms + _off_threads) * get_max_nbors() * sizeof(int);
 + tmem += (bigint)(_list_alloc_atoms + _off_threads) * (bigint)get_max_nbors() * sizeof(int);
 tmem += _ntypes * _ntypes * sizeof(int);

 tmem += _buf_local_size + (_n_list_ptrs - 1) * _buf_local_size * 2;
